  "worked_solution": "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-3\\pi $ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi +2\\pi  & =-\\pi  \\\\ -3\\pi +2\\cdot 2\\pi  & =\\pi \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-3\\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\pi .$\n\nThe angle $\\pi $ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266176/assets/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\pi $ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =-1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\pi =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }=\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-3\\pi )=-1.$",
